  The reference `--follow` inspector emits only validated changed snapshots at
  a fixed one-second poll cadence. It admits sampled checkpoint-generation
  jumps and short-lived `queued -> running -> checkpointed/completed` state
  changes between polls, while rejecting backward sequence, timing/unit
  regression, stable-identity drift, and same-generation payload-reference
  changes. Writer transitions remain strict and terminal state is emitted once.

  Typed-IR and ternary-lowering composition now each expose one explicit
  Fresh-versus-Progress selector. Fresh input enters the existing stage
  admission directly; Progress input delegates durable extraction to the trusted
  inspector and preserves a distinct selected-route failure category before
  re-admission. Integration regressions prove fresh and progress paths converge
  on the same admitted stage state.

  Typed-IR inspector transport now rejects an early nonzero exit without
  waiting for descendants holding stdout/stderr open; a three-second inherited-
  pipe regression requires prompt failure instead of a deadline-sized wait.

  Both stage transports now reject bare interpreter executable names
  before host PATH lookup. Rust integration regressions confirm the launch
  failure category rather than accepting an unrelated zero-exit executable.
  Both stage suites also admit explicitly directory-qualified relative Python
  paths and preserve the same complete stdout payload.

  Python inspector script arguments now use the option terminator, preventing
  interpreter flags such as `-V` from substituting for a trusted script.
  The lone `-` stdin-script special case rejects before child launch.
  Both stage transports also require Python isolated mode before inspecting,
  with subprocess checks of the isolated interpreter startup flag.
  Linux subprocess fixtures poison PYTHONHOME and PYTHONPATH while requiring
  unchanged checkpoint bytes and no injected sitecustomize filesystem writes.

  Both Linux startup-isolation wrappers are now installed by a child process
  from non-executable sources to prevent writable interpreter inode handles
  from leaking into concurrent test forks. The default-threaded compiler-IR
  suite passed 50 repetitions after this isolation, versus two failures in
  25 repetitions before the change; the inspector's production launch and
  validation policies are unaffected.

  Both inspector transports now reject completed nonempty diagnostics
  without waiting for unrelated stdout pipe holders; independent Linux
  regressions require fast rejection from zero-exit inspector children.
  Delayed-marker fixtures also prove that neither inspector leaves an
  inherited stdout descendant alive after rejecting diagnostic output.

  The typed-IR and ternary checkpoint extractors now isolate their Linux
  inspector children into private process groups and kill ordinary inherited-
  pipe descendants on deadline failure. Independent subprocess regressions
  verify that neither child can leave a delayed filesystem side effect after
  timeout; detached sessions and non-Linux process trees remain out of scope.

  Pointer preflight for direct sidecar and generation publication now uses
  a non-following metadata probe, admitting only a genuinely missing pointer
  and rejecting permission errors before publishing immutable state or JSON.
  Regression coverage also preserves an already committed predecessor when
  metadata admission fails for either publication route.

  Deeply nested valid JSON that overflows Python's JSON scanner is now
  converted to a stable sidecar or checkpoint-envelope error rather than a
  raw recursion traceback. A CLI regression also verifies no partial output.

  Source provenance pathname validation now rejects NUL, unencodable
  surrogate text, and directory-only names before pointer publication,
  without requiring that the source file exist or changing resume identity.

  Progress pointer and checkpoint generation readers now require exact native
  `os.stat_result` receipts for every filename/descriptor probe across open,
  post-read and single-hard-link admission. Sixteen adversarial read-phase
  regressions reject foreign stat objects before reading any forged property;
  ordinary canonical and checkpoint reads preserve their existing behavior.

  Durable pointer and immutable payload writers now reject foreign staging
  descriptor receipts before inspecting their fields or acquiring staging
  ownership. Both publishers also reject forged metadata at the initial and
  post-read staging inode/size probes. Ten adversarial phase regressions check
  typed failures, closed descriptors and absent final artifacts without
  broadening the stage's persistence semantics.

  POSIX parent-directory durability checks now admit exact native descriptor
  and no-follow path stat receipts at both before-fsync and after-fsync phases.
  Eight red-first pointer/checkpoint publication regressions reject foreign
  receipts with typed uncertain-durability errors and exact committed paths;
  unrelated checkpoint metadata and final artifacts remain unchanged.

  Process-shared writer locks now require native stat results across the
  existing path preflight, opened descriptor and acquired inode identity.
  Exclusive single-link lock ownership is now required both after lock
  acquisition and after staging, with red-first external hard-link and
  deterministic late-alias rejection before pointer publication.
  Four adversarial lock fixtures prove every foreign receipt rejects before
  its fields are accessed, without advancing publication or altering
  positive native locking behavior.

  Both POSIX and Windows writer-lock system calls now admit only the native
  `None` acquisition and release receipt. Four red-first POSIX cases show a
  boolean or integer acquisition claim cannot bypass serialization; an invalid
  release claim preserves the durably committed pointer and raises exact
  committed-path evidence. A portable Windows branch harness additionally
  rejects six forged CRT locking receipts and six falsified lock-stream seek
  positions at acquisition or release; exact zero-position and native `None`
  completion remain admissible. Eleven additional red-first cases verify
  native nonnegative descriptor receipts before lock acquisition and at the
  post-lock inode recheck, plus committed-path retention for a forged release
  callback completion. Two additional red-first overflow cases constrain
  descriptor receipts to the native C `int` range before calling OS primitives.
  Positive native flock locking remains covered by the
  existing cross-process contention and process-death tests. Two further
  red-first fixtures prove a matching native metadata receipt with a forged
  opened-directory or opened-FIFO type cannot authorize lock admission.

  Regular payload-file and POSIX parent-directory synchronization now also
  requires the native `None` `fsync` completion receipt. Eight red-first cases
  reject forged boolean/integer file sync results before pointer or checkpoint
  publication, while directory-sync rejection preserves the exact committed
  pointer or checkpoint path as uncertain durability evidence. Existing real
  crash and process-restart fixtures continue to cover native sync behavior.
  Six red-first pointer/checkpoint cases reject forged staged payload flush
  completion receipts before immutable or mutable publication.
  Two red-first forged-stream fixtures additionally prove that syncing an
  unrelated, valid regular-file descriptor cannot authorize checkpoint or
  pointer publication, even if all staged bytes and metadata match.

  Immutable checkpoint collision and initial post-link publication checks
  now validate native stat metadata before reading inode, type or link count.
  Five red-first regressions cover new committed inode uncertainty and
  collision preflight/reuse rejection while preserving existing bytes.

  Retained immutable staging-alias recovery now requires native directory
  entry, destination and alias stat receipts; foreign evidence cannot authorize
  alias cleanup. Mutable and immutable failed-write staging cleanup also rejects
  untrusted inode receipts without masking the primary write error or unlinking
  unverified files. Five adversarial cases exercise these boundaries.

  A top-level C-to-Malbolge compiler entrypoint does not yet exist, so
  cross-stage product CLI resume selection, compiler-stage codecs beyond ternary
  lowering, crash injection beyond the ternary stage and final-artifact
  publication, and later compiler-execution CPU/CUDA resume equivalence remain
  open; this TODO is therefore active.
- Required durable artifacts: versioned JSON schema, atomic writer, checkpoint
  compatibility fingerprint, CLI/service integration, injected-clock tests,
  crash fixtures, CPU/CUDA resume differential evidence, and timing reports.
- The resident compiler WAL may reuse this job-level contract but cannot replace
  it with process-private state.
- Prerequisite completion evidence: experiment identity, target profile
  identity, benchmark/statistical evidence protocol, and documentation gate.
